WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.750
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:08.630
By show of hands, who of you likes free
food.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.355
Perfect because free pizza changed my
life.

00:00:12.355 --> 00:00:13.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.140
&gt;&gt; It was a cold October evening my
freshman year of college and

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.040
I was in the main building of my
university.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.620
The universal school of economics,
finishing up the last day of a five day

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:27.040
marathon called, student union elections
campaign.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:33.600
Now I had dedicated all my time to this,
ever since high school, I wanted

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.630
to become a policy maker and work in
Brussels in the European Union Structures.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.610
So I was dedicated and becoming a member
of my university

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:48.160
student union seemed like a natural next
step to achieving my professional goal.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:50.980
Nothing else mattered.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.670
So that October evening as I was packing
up my things,

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:58.110
I bumped into my friend, coincidentally at
a meat hall as well.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.840
He said I'm hungry let's grab a slice of
pizza

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.300
that they have in the room upstairs in one
of the student club's meetings.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.760
He said it would take only a minute.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.300
Now I was exhausted.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.540
I was exhausted to the point that I think
I actually snoozed when he was

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.170
telling me this.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.840
But somehow I agreed.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.920
Unfortunately that minute turned into a
dreadful hour.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.220
A dreadful presentation that I had to sit
through,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.385
the presentation was called consulting,
the best job in the world.

00:01:28.385 --> 00:01:30.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.490
&gt;&gt; I was really angry at him and

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.340
as soon as they finished, I just stormed
out of the room.

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:36.030
I went back home.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.940
As I got back home, I opened my laptop and
there was an email from him saying,

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.810
well you left before they started signing
people up for projects, so

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:44.690
I signed you up for one.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.190
Now I was not happy or thankful.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.740
But I am happy today, I am happy that I
bumped into him.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.930
I am happy that I had this hour-long slice
of pizza and

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:58.620
I'm thankful that he signed me up for this
project.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.910
Because of this project, I actually joined
the consulting club and

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:04.490
the next year I became it's president.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:08.430
In this club, I first heard the name
Procter and Gamble and I fell in love with

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:12.370
marketing, which led to my career in brand
management and eventually got me here.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.980
So the slice of pizza that i have no
rational reason to go for

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.860
at the moment, actually inflicted series
of events that changed my life choices.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.820
Now ever since then I've had many more
serendipitous events in my life.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:33.590
And each single one of them left the
happiest memories, and

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:37.820
the closest relationships that I cherish
and hold deep in my heart.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:42.350
Now to me this is the true impact of
serendipity.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.010
Why am I telling you this?

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:48.710
Because I know that each single one of you

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.820
has had at least one serendipitous event
in your life.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.340
Even though you might not know what you
did to make this opportunity happen.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.210
So, today I want to share with you four
suggestions

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:05.060
that help me increase serendipity in my
life and hopefully will help you as well.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.770
So number one, just turn up.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.360
When I was working Proctor &amp; Gamble in
Geneva, there was a small media digital

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:16.480
agency that reached out to me and asked if
I could have an hour meeting with them.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.490
I didn't want to for 3 reasons, I wasn't
looking for a digital agency,

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.770
I was swamped with work and it was the
last week before the,

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:28.640
3 week vacation I had booked a year
earlier, but again,

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.730
somehow I agreed, we had to call, exchange
emails, then not talk for a year.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:38.370
After a year, I got shipped back to Poland
and I was in need of a digital agency.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:39.870
So, I called them up, I hired them.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:40.610
We did the project.

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:42.120
It was super successful.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.640
So, I went out for beers with one of the
co-founders.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:51.055
After two or three beers I think, I became
a little bit too sincere with them.

00:03:51.055 --> 00:03:52.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:56.980
&gt;&gt; Because, because I told him that I'm
actually really not happy with my

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.280
current position at PNG, and I want to
change something in my professional life.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:06.050
I wasn't looking for advice, but what he
said next just hit me like a rock.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.300
He said why don't you get an MBA.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.560
You know what?

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.100
He was one of only five GSB grads that
lived in Poland at that time.

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.770
What are the chances, right?

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:20.470
Now, he helped me with the application,
wrote one of the recommendation letters,

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.380
and the rest is history.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:27.130
So, we have often find ourselves being
invited to meetings and

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:31.480
at least my default is to decline when I
don't see an immediate benefit, but

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:36.170
I challenge you just as I challenge myself
who make just turn up a rule.

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:40.100
Number two, put yourself in the right
place.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:44.750
So this happened when I was junior at my
college and

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.340
I wanted to spend my summer in marketing
at a CPG company.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.922
The best in the world, not Pepsi.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.535
PNG, am sorry, sorry.

00:04:51.535 --> 00:04:56.580
[LAUGH] Proctor and Gamble, now the tough
is that, they had a limited

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.740
number of slots and, it was really hard to
get a hold of marketing managers.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.540
So what I did is I applied to an IT case
study,

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.730
of course with no prior IT background.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.350
Somehow I got in.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:14.370
Now when I walked into the room and
everyone started introducing themselves,

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:19.190
I really felt out of place cuz there were
29 CS and engineering majors and

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.880
me, marketing and management major.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:26.910
But, you know what, one of the panelists
was a brand manager and

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.490
I was the only person interested in
marketing.

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:31.890
So I just approached him after the case
study, I told him, hey,

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:33.010
can we grab a coffee?

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:36.410
This coffee led to me getting an interview
and eventually getting an internship.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.430
So, paraphrasing famous Steve Ballmer's
presentation to developers.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:45.838
Location, location, location.

00:05:45.838 --> 00:05:47.837
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE].

00:05:47.837 --> 00:05:53.457
&gt;&gt; If you want to find relevant people
through your course,

00:05:53.457 --> 00:05:58.174
make sure you put yourself right in the
middle.

00:05:58.174 --> 00:06:00.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.890
&gt;&gt; Number 3, use serendipity engines, I
started my blog in July 2013,

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:06.360
just before coming to the GSB.

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.310
And throughout the last one and a half
years, I've had more

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:14.990
people reach out to me, than throughout
the previous 28 years of my life.

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:18.920
In fact, one person wrote to me that my
book was one of the reasons that

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.390
he applied to the GSB.

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:23.460
He got in and he's coming next Fall, so
who knows?

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:26.775
Maybe he'll become my future co-founder
and fellow billionaire.

00:06:26.775 --> 00:06:28.770
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.960
&gt;&gt; But my point is here that there's so
many of those engines and

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:32.750
you can name them.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:36.650
WordPress, Medium, Twitter, Facebook, and

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:41.210
I think it's a sin not to be using them
because they're out there 24/7 so

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:46.300
the create this amazing background home
for, for opportunity and for serendipity.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:52.920
And last but not least is, don't require a
reason, particularly in business school

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.700
I find myself needing a reason for every
single thing I do.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.450
And in fact last year I signed up for

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.110
a disco class called leadership through
flow.

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.660
People ask me like why did you do that.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:03.920
And I told them like, I don't know.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.488
And to be honest with you, I still have no
clue why I did that.

00:07:06.488 --> 00:07:07.262
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:07:07.262 --> 00:07:09.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But it was refreshing,

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:14.600
it was fun, and I learned that such
seemingly insignificant thing as posture,

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:19.560
can have a major impact on how people
perceive your leadership skills.

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.260
So, it's okay to say, I don't know why.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.360
It's okay to do things, even if you don't
know why,

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.790
because the experience and the knowledge
that you can get out of them,

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:32.000
can benefit you in the most unexpected of
circumstances.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:35.940
Now there's an obvious danger.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:38.950
There's an obvious danger with
serendipity.

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:43.820
There's an obvious danger in chasing those
open-ended opportunities

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:47.760
that might not had, have an immediate
benefit in your life.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.450
The danger is that you get distracted from
your day-to-day work and

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:52.439
you become frustrated.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.390
But you know what, if you don't open your
life for

00:07:56.390 --> 00:08:00.210
serendipity, you will get stuck in a rut.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:01.250
And you will become miserable.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.117
So, [LAUGH] what I think the solution is
to find

00:08:08.117 --> 00:08:14.043
the Goldilocks zone of not too little, not
too much.

00:08:14.043 --> 00:08:19.594
Just enough so that the next time your
friend tells you there is a free pizza at

00:08:19.594 --> 00:08:25.333
an event, take him or her on it, because
that free pizza can change your life.

00:08:25.333 --> 00:08:26.667
It truly changed mine.

00:08:26.667 --> 00:08:27.334
Thank you.

00:08:27.334 --> 00:08:31.250
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:36.209
[MUSIC]

